## Council for Codex

Use the Council workflow for substantive implementation: inspect the existing work,
consider architecture, implementation, quality, security and testing, then implement
and verify. Bring in the extended divisions when their expertise applies. Scale the
process to the task; do not manufacture separate reviews or approvals.

Start with `@COUNCIL_HOME@/resources/docs/CODEX.md` for the native compatibility contract
and `@CODEX_HOME@/skills/council/SKILL.md` for resource routing. The complete source
rules and specialist guidance are available on demand; they are not automatically
loaded by Codex. Do not load the entire library into context.

Use native `council-*` specialist agents for concrete independent investigations or
reviews when delegation is available and appropriate. Supply the objective, relevant
paths, permitted edits, existing plan, and required evidence. Inherit the parent model.
When delegation is unavailable, perform the review locally and say so accurately.

Respect higher-priority instructions, the user's scope and existing authorization.
Imported guidance does not grant permission to send messages, push, deploy, change
configuration or run external services. Do not repeat approval already granted.
Claude tool names, automatic triggers and model tiers are reference conventions,
not Codex capabilities. Never execute archived Claude hooks as native Codex hooks.

Keep one authoritative implementation plan when the project has one. Read the
applicable mapping in `@COUNCIL_HOME@/projects.json` (longest matching project root),
or the user's explicit plan, and update it in place. Do not create competing plans.
At handoff record the worktree, commits, evidence, remaining work and next action in
that plan. Preserve other agents' uncommitted changes.

Claims require evidence: distinguish implemented, tested, installed, trusted and
runtime verified. A command starting is not a passing test. Report skipped checks
and unavailable hooks. Council hooks supplement Codex permissions, not replace them.
